A throwing `new` uses the address space that is kept aside.

An embedder that keeps address space aside (ask-who-may-take-address-space.patch)
lets go of it for requests that cannot fail, and knows of its own which those
are. A throwing `operator new` in a build without exceptions is one as well,
and only mimalloc sees it.

The keeper gets two more functions. When a throwing `new` has found no memory,
begin_request_that_cannot_fail() is called on its thread, the request asks once
more, and end_request_that_cannot_fail() is called. What follows when that
finds no memory either is as before. `new (std::nothrow)` is as before.

